/**
 * File-backed NonceStore: the same replay guard as InMemoryNonceStore, but the
 * claims survive a restart.
 *
 * WHY A FILE AND NOT A DATABASE. `NonceStore.claim` needs one atomic
 * compare-and-swap and nothing else. `updateVersioned` in
 * @celo/buy-keystore already provides exactly that — lock-free, crash-safe,
 * and already carrying the spend ledger, which is the same shape of problem —
 * so this costs no new dependency, no native module, and no build step. SQLite
 * would have added all three to buy the same single-host guarantee, since
 * neither it nor a file solves the multi-instance case.
 *
 * WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO. State is a file, so the guarantee is per FILESYSTEM.
 * Two instances sharing a disk are safe (the CAS is a `link()`, which is atomic
 * across processes); two instances in separate containers are NOT — each keeps
 * its own claims and a replay can be accepted once per instance. Horizontally
 * scaled deployments need a shared store; the interface is async precisely so
 * a Redis implementation (`SET key token NX PX ttl`) can slot in unchanged.
 *
 * COST. Every claim and release is a read-modify-write of one JSON file, so
 * this is heavier than the in-memory store by roughly a file round-trip per
 * verification. That is deliberate: it buys durability on the path where a
 * lost claim means an accepted replay. Deployments that would rather have the
 * speed keep `InMemoryNonceStore`.
 */
